{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Dedicated AST Indexer|\\\\\"'$ast|occurrence relation|7'\"\\\\.1' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"8:provenance-free; `dao.space.query/q` runs over the rows directly; all `yin.vm` evaluators are `dao.stream` observers that load rows to evaluate code; concurrently, a dedicated AST indexer observes the same stream and maintains the **row relation** of §6.1 (the `$ast` relation `q` runs over) from the rows it observes. `dao.jing` is the content store the rows already live in; it is not the indexer's output; code on a stream is rows end to end; `t` and `m` live in a ledger of\n60:|                |                                | and the occurrence relation (§6.1)  |                    |                          |                                |\n77:and the occurrence relation (`§6.1`) — and wherever this document says\n99:address is), not the load path (loaders take content directly, `§7.1`), and\n161:(§7.1) — and it is ephemeral and per-consumer, like an image: each\n648:joins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\n666:is free or bound is exactly what a **query** over `$ast` (§6.1) can\n729:§6.1 already routes those through the occurrence relation\n735:hand-built occurrence relation of §2.5's shape (the production indexer\n748:is harmless, but a production occurrence relation holds every reachable\n919:over parent slots or over the occurrence relation below, never over\n922:**The Dedicated AST Indexer:** This row relation (called `$ast` in queries) is maintained by the dedicated AST indexer (§1). The indexer is a `dao.stream` observer peer to the evaluators (§7.1); its input is the row batches of §7.1; its output is this row relation plus the occurrence relation below; it is distinct from `dao.space.index`, which indexes datoms only (§6.5); it is a projection keeper per §1's Query layer, so discarding it loses nothing.\n929:`rows → map` is **load-time reconstruction** (§7.1): validate the rows\n941:sound, but a places question still needs the occurrence relation, not id\n1021:address. The result names places, not contents: the occurrence relation\n1029:(`v2.cljc:372-482`) and is the *projection path* of §7.1. It exists for\n1050:### 7.1 Load paths\n1231:Extraction over trees (`$ast` is the union of flat rows of the reachable\n1251:[:find ?name :in $ast $occ % ?root\n1256:[:find ?key :in $ast\n1258:                 [$ast _ :vm/store-get ?key]\n1259:                 [$ast _ :vm/store-put ?key _]\n1260:                 (and [$ast _ :application ?op ?operands _]\n1261:                      [$ast ?op :variable ?name]\n1264:                      [$ast ?kid :literal ?key]))]\n1267:[:find ?op :in $ast :where [$ast _ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]]\n1270:[:find ?pid :in $ast :where [$ast _ :vm/resume ?pid _]]\n1273:[:find ?tag :in $ast\n1274: :where [$ast _ ?tag & _]\n1308:#### 7.7.1 Effect normalization: the footprint table\n1380:`$ast`/`$code`, not a per-tag footprint fact, and is computed by the\n1423:`:define` instruction, whose write is machine state (S7.7.1), and it is\n1467:(§7.7.1), and the callable and module footprints. A pass that adds a new\n2016:  a medium contract this design has not exercised end to end; §7.1 assumes\n2107:6. **Effect normalization** (§7.7.1): the footprint table must be\n2132:11. **The primitive-profile registry** — inherited from UCF §7.11. Every\n2154:    `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.11's contract-revision-\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
