{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc | head -240; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc | head -270'","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yin.repl.index\n     2\t  \"The shell's code indexer: a `dao.space.index` observer on `program-out`\n     3\t   (docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md).\n     4\t\n     5\t   It is a peer of the evaluator's observer on the same medium, attached\n     6\t   independently and advanced by the evaluation round, never by the\n     7\t   evaluator: neither knows the other.  Each expanded tree packet `[root\n     8\t   rows]` it observes is projected to local `[e a v t m]` AST facts\n     9\t   (yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md §6.5) and committed as ONE atomic transaction\n    10\t   record through a `dao.space.transactor`, which allocates `t`; this\n    11\t   namespace supplies no clock.  After a round that committed, the covered\n    12\t   indexes are published through `transactor/publish!` into that round's\n    13\t   own fresh intake, the intake is drained into the `dao.jing` store, the\n    14\t   manifest is read back, and the intake is dropped: between rounds the\n    15\t   indexer retains its local transaction log and nothing of any\n    16\t   publication.\n    17\t\n    18\t   Cost, accepted as the price of the owner's every-round cadence\n    19\t   (2026-09-28): each publication rebuilds the covered indexes over the\n    20\t   whole retained history, and opening the round's transactor rescans\n    21\t   that history to derive `t`, so a round's index time grows linearly with\n    22\t   the session's committed code and a session's total grows\n    23\t   quadratically.  Memory does not: at most one publication is held, and\n    24\t   only during its round.  An incremental publish in `dao.space.index`\n    25\t   would remove the time growth; nothing here would need to change but\n    26\t   the call.\n    27\t\n    28\t   Every `e` is a transactor-local entity id; the only durable name in the\n    29\t   projection is the `:yin/address` fact each node occurrence carries back\n    30\t   to its row address.  `m` is a local metadata entity, one per program,\n    31\t   carrying `:db/op`, the shell's stable session token, the program's root\n    32\t   address, and the round.  Only code is indexed: no printed output, last\n    33\t   value, or evaluation result enters.\n    34\t\n    35\t   Loss is reported, never hidden, and never stops the evaluator (owner\n    36\t   ruling, 2026-09-28): a gap is counted on the observer (`:ingress-gaps`)\n    37\t   and marks the indexer `:lost?`, after which it consumes packets without\n    38\t   indexing any until reset rebuilds it; a packet that could not be\n    39\t   committed, or a publication that did not become readable, is recorded\n    40\t   under `:failure`, never counted as indexed.  `notice` renders what a\n    41\t   round lost for the shell's round result, and `status` is the\n    42\t   evaluator-independent view `repl-state` reports.  Everything here is a\n    43\t   session-composition value; reset and VM selection rebuild it with the\n    44\t   session.\"\n    45\t  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n    46\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n    47\t            [dao.space.index :as index]\n    48\t            [dao.space.transactor :as transactor]\n    49\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\n    50\t            [dao.stream.memory-log :as memory-log]\n    51\t            [dao.stream.observer :as observer]\n    52\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n    53\t            [yin.vm.macro :as macro]))\n    54\t\n    55\t\n    56\t;; =============================================================================\n    57\t;; The §6.5 projection\n    58\t;; =============================================================================\n    59\t\n    60\t(defn- assign-local-ids\n    61\t  \"Give every node occurrence of `node`, the map AST rebuilt from row `id`,\n    62\t   a fresh local entity id as its `:eid`, pairing it with its row through\n    63\t   the §2.3 slot table.  Distinct occurrences of one shared row get distinct\n    64\t   ids, so `vm/ast->datoms` emits every occurrence exactly as it does for a\n    65\t   tree without `:eid`s.  `acc` is `[next-e addresses]`; answers\n    66\t   `[node' acc']`, `addresses` mapping each id to its row address.\"\n    67\t  [rows node id [next-e addresses]]\n    68\t  (let [row (get rows id)\n    69\t        slots (get vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar (:type node))\n    70\t        child (fn [acc child-node child-id]\n    71\t                (assign-local-ids rows child-node child-id acc))]\n    72\t    (reduce\n    73\t      (fn [[node acc] [i [field kind]]]\n    74\t        (let [slot (nth row (+ 2 i))]\n    75\t          (case kind\n    76\t            :node (let [[c acc'] (child acc (get node field) slot)]\n    77\t                    [(assoc node field c) acc'])\n    78\t            :nodes (let [[cs acc']\n    79\t                         (reduce (fn [[cs acc] [c cid]]\n    80\t                                   (let [[c' acc'] (child acc c cid)]\n    81\t                                     [(conj cs c') acc']))\n    82\t                                 [[] acc]\n    83\t                                 (map vector (get node field) slot))]\n    84\t                     [(assoc node field cs) acc'])\n    85\t            [node acc])))\n    86\t      [(assoc node :eid next-e) [(inc next-e) (assoc addresses next-e id)]]\n    87\t      (map-indexed vector slots))))\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t(defn packet->tx-data\n    91\t  \"Project one expanded tree packet to transactor tx-data, allocating local\n    92\t   entity ids from `next-e`.  `provenance` is `{:session-token s :round r}`.\n    93\t\n    94\t   Answers `{:tx-data [...] :next-e n :meta-e m :root root}`: the metadata\n    95\t   entity's facts, then `vm/ast->datoms` of the expanded tree under `m`,\n    96\t   then one `[e :yin/address addr]` per node occurrence.  Every datom leaves\n    97\t   `t` nil, which the transactor stamps.\"\n    98\t  [packet next-e {:keys [session-token round]}]\n    99\t  (let [{:keys [root rows], :as row-set} (macro/packet->row-set packet)\n   100\t        meta-e next-e\n   101\t        [ast [next-e' addresses]] (assign-local-ids\n   102\t                                    rows\n   103\t                                    (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast row-set)\n   104\t                                    root\n   105\t                                    [(inc meta-e) {}])\n   106\t        facts (vm/ast->datoms ast {:m meta-e})\n   107\t        untimed (fn [e a v m] [e a v nil m])]\n   108\t    (when-let [stray (some (fn [[e]] (when (neg? e) e)) facts)]\n   109\t      (throw (ex-info \"projected node escaped local id assignment\"\n   110\t                      {:e stray, :root root})))\n   111\t    {:tx-data (-> [(untimed meta-e :db/op :db/assert datom/default-op)\n   112\t                   (untimed meta-e :yin.repl/session session-token\n   113\t                            datom/default-op)\n   114\t                   (untimed meta-e :yin.repl/root root datom/default-op)\n   115\t                   (untimed meta-e :yin.repl/round round datom/default-op)]\n   116\t                  (into (map (fn [[e a v _t m]] (untimed e a v m))) facts)\n   117\t                  (into (map (fn [[e addr]]\n   118\t                               (untimed e :yin/address addr meta-e)))\n   119\t                        (sort-by key addresses))),\n   120\t     :next-e next-e',\n   121\t     :meta-e meta-e,\n   122\t     :root root}))\n   123\t\n   124\t\n   125\t;; =============================================================================\n   126\t;; The indexer value\n   127\t;; =============================================================================\n   128\t\n   129\t(defn- memory-log\n   130\t  []\n   131\t  (:dao.stream/handle\n   132\t    (memory-log/create! {:dao.stream/type memory-log/transport-type})))\n   133\t\n   134\t\n   135\t(defn make-indexer\n   136\t  \"The indexer for one session: `observer`, attached to `program-out`\n   137\t   beside the evaluator's; `:local`, the complete-retention log its\n   138\t   transactions are committed to (dao.space.transactor requires memory-log\n   139\t   retention); and `content-store`, the `dao.jing` byte store publications\n   140\t   materialize into.  `session-token` is the shell's stable token, recorded\n   141\t   as provenance; `publish-opts` reach `transactor/publish!` as-is.\n   142\t\n   143\t   Between rounds that is all it holds.  A round that has code to commit\n   144\t   opens its `:publication` — a transactor over `:local` whose intake pool\n   145\t   is one fresh complete-retention intake — and drops it when the round\n   146\t   ends, so no earlier publication's payloads stay reachable from the\n   147\t   indexer (see `step`).\"\n   148\t  [{:keys [observer session-token content-store publish-opts]}]\n   149\t  {:observer observer\n   150\t   :local (memory-log)\n   151\t   :content-store content-store\n   152\t   :session-token session-token\n   153\t   :publish-opts publish-opts\n   154\t   :next-e datom/first-user-id\n   155\t   :transactions 0\n   156\t   :published 0\n   157\t   :published-payloads 0\n   158\t   :manifest-address nil\n   159\t   :lost? false\n   160\t   :failure nil})\n   161\t\n   162\t\n   163\t(defn- open-publication\n   164\t  \"The round's transactor and the `dao.jing` pool observer over its intake.\n   165\t   `transactor/create!` derives the next `t` from `:local`'s retained\n   166\t   history, so transaction time continues across rounds.  The intake is\n   167\t   complete-retention: `publish-index!` appends every node blob before the\n   168\t   manifest and the round drains only after it returns, so an evicting\n   169\t   intake would lose the head of any publication larger than its capacity\n   170\t   and that publication could never complete.\"\n   171\t  [ix]\n   172\t  (if (:publication ix)\n   173\t    ix\n   174\t    (let [intake (memory-log)]\n   175\t      (assoc ix\n   176\t             :publication\n   177\t             {:transactor (transactor/create! {:local-stream (:local ix)\n   178\t                                               :intake-pool [intake]\n   179\t                                               :name \"yin.repl.index\"})\n   180\t              :pool (jing/observer-state\n   181\t                      [{:stream intake\n   182\t                        :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor\n   183\t                                  (stream/cursor intake\n   184\t                                                 :dao.stream/oldest))}])}))))\n   185\t\n   186\t\n   187\t(defn- commit\n   188\t  \"Commit one packet as one transaction.  A packet that could not be\n   189\t   projected or committed is consumed and recorded as the failure, never\n   190\t   counted.\"\n   191\t  [ix packet round]\n   192\t  (try\n   193\t    (let [{:keys [tx-data next-e root]}\n   194\t          (packet->tx-data packet\n   195\t                           (:next-e ix)\n   196\t                           {:session-token (:session-token ix), :round round})\n   197\t          answer (transactor/transact! (get-in ix [:publication :transactor])\n   198\t                                       tx-data)]\n   199\t      (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome answer))\n   200\t        [(-> ix\n   201\t             (assoc :next-e next-e)\n   202\t             (update :transactions inc))\n   203\t         true]\n   204\t        [(assoc ix\n   205\t                :failure {:round round\n   206\t                          :root root\n   207\t                          :stage :transact\n   208\t                          :outcome (:dao.stream/outcome answer)})\n   209\t         false]))\n   210\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) e\n   211\t      [(assoc ix\n   212\t              :failure {:round round\n   213\t                        :stage :project\n   214\t                        :message (ex-message e)})\n   215\t       false])))\n   216\t\n   217\t\n   218\t(defn- materialize\n   219\t  \"Drain the intake into the store until it blocks.  Answers how many\n   220\t   payloads it materialized and, when the drain met a gap or a defect, what\n   221\t   it met.  The pool is not kept: its intake is dropped with the round.\"\n   222\t  [content-store pool]\n   223\t  (loop [pool pool\n   224\t         n 0]\n   225\t    (let [{:keys [signal], :as r} (jing/observe-step! content-store pool)]\n   226\t      (case signal\n   227\t        :dao.stream/ok (recur (:state r) (inc n))\n   228\t        :dao.stream/blocked [n nil]\n   229\t        [n signal]))))\n   230\t\n   231\t\n   232\t(defn- publish\n   233\t  \"Publish the covered indexes over every committed transaction through\n   234\t   the round's transactor, drain its intake into the store, and read the\n   235\t   manifest back from it.  Only a publication whose manifest the store\n   236\t   answers becomes the reported one, covering `:published` transactions.\"\n   237\t  [ix round]\n   238\t  (try\n   239\t    (let [{:keys [transactor pool]} (:publication ix)\n   240\t          {:keys [manifest-address]} (transactor/publish! transactor\n     1\t(ns yin.repl.query\n     2\t  \"The shell's query bridge: `(require 'dao.space.query)` activates\n     3\t   `dao.space.query/q` over the session's code index\n     4\t   (docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md, \\\"Query surface\\\").\n     5\t\n     6\t   `dao.space.query` is a session-scoped host module that is absent from\n     7\t   the VM's registry until the user requires it.  The session's registry\n     8\t   answers `:module/require` for that one name by installing the module\n     9\t   into the requiring VM's own registry value, and hands every other name\n    10\t   to `yin.vm.module/require-handler` unchanged.  Before the require, the\n    11\t   qualified export fails name resolution like any unknown name.  A session\n    12\t   rebuild (`(reset)`, `(vm ...)`) builds a fresh registry, which drops the\n    13\t   module.\n    14\t\n    15\t   The export `q` is a data constructor: it returns a `::call` effect\n    16\t   carrying its arguments, and the session registry's handler for that\n    17\t   effect parks the calling continuation as an ordinary FFI call on the\n    18\t   VM's `dao.stream.apply` call pair.  The call uses the VM's own park id,\n    19\t   caller-scoped call id and response reader (`yin.vm.ffi`), so the engine's\n    20\t   FFI response router wakes the call and `ffi/call-result` unwraps the\n    21\t   answer or raises its error, exactly as for a `dao.stream.apply/call`\n    22\t   node.  The request carries only data: the query, its `:in` inputs, and\n    23\t   an optional final options map.\n    24\t\n    25\t   The shell owns the other end of the pair.  `serve` reads each request\n    26\t   and answers it from the indexer the shell holds when it serves, never\n    27\t   from state captured when the session was built: the snapshot is the\n    28\t   latest manifest the indexer published and read back.  A lost indexer,\n    29\t   a recorded failure, or committed code not yet published refuses the\n    30\t   call, because an older manifest must not be presented as complete.  A\n    31\t   session that has committed nothing is a valid empty database.\n    32\t\n    33\t   Answers and refusals are portable data.  Refusals use the FFI error\n    34\t   envelope under a stable code: `::index-unavailable`, `::invalid-input`,\n    35\t   `::result-limit`, or `::query-failed` for a query the engine rejects.\n    36\t   Limits are the caller's: `serve` takes the row and encoded-byte bound\n    37\t   and checks both before it appends a response.\"\n    38\t  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:typed_data\" :refer [Uint8List]]]\n    39\t                :default [])\n    40\t            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n    41\t            [dao.space.index :as index]\n    42\t            [dao.space.query :as query]\n    43\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\n    44\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]\n    45\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]\n    46\t            [yin.repl.index :as repl.index]\n    47\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n    48\t            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n    49\t            [yin.vm.ffi :as ffi]\n    50\t            [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(def module-name\n    54\t  \"The module `(require 'dao.space.query)` activates.\"\n    55\t  'dao.space.query)\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(def op\n    59\t  \"The `dao.stream.apply` operation a `q` call requests.\"\n    60\t  ::q)\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t(def views\n    64\t  \"The options map's `:view` values and the `dao.space.query` interpreter\n    65\t   each names.  `:current` is the default.\"\n    66\t  {:current query/current\n    67\t   :history query/history})\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t;; =============================================================================\n    71\t;; The module and its call\n    72\t;; =============================================================================\n    73\t\n    74\t(defn- q\n    75\t  \"The export: a data constructor.  The effect it returns is answered by\n    76\t   `call-handler`, which parks the caller on the call pair.\"\n    77\t  [& args]\n    78\t  {:effect ::call, :args (vec args)})\n    79\t\n    80\t\n    81\t(def ^:private exports\n    82\t  {'q q})\n    83\t\n    84\t\n    85\t(def ^:private profiles\n    86\t  {'q (vm/primitive-profile 'q :effectful [1 2 :variadic] #{::call} :none)})\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t(defn activate\n    90\t  \"`registry` with the `dao.space.query` host module installed.\"\n    91\t  [registry]\n    92\t  (module/register-host-module registry module-name exports profiles))\n    93\t\n    94\t\n    95\t(defn- require-handler\n    96\t  \"`:module/require` for a session with the query bridge: the first\n    97\t   require of `dao.space.query` installs the module into the requiring\n    98\t   VM's registry value and answers at once; every other require, and a\n    99\t   repeated one, is `module/require-handler`'s.\"\n   100\t  [state effect opts]\n   101\t  (if (and (= module-name (:module effect))\n   102\t           (nil? (module/resolve-module (:modules state) module-name)))\n   103\t    {:value module-name,\n   104\t     :state (update state :modules activate),\n   105\t     :blocked? false}\n   106\t    (module/require-handler state effect opts)))\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\t(defn- blocked\n   110\t  [state entry]\n   111\t  {:state (-> state\n   112\t              (update :wait-set (fnil conj []) entry)\n   113\t              (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n   114\t                     :blocked? true\n   115\t                     :halted? false)),\n   116\t   :value :yin/blocked,\n   117\t   :blocked? true})\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\t(defn- call-handler\n   121\t  \"Park the continuation of a `q` call as an FFI call and append its\n   122\t   request.  The continuation's registers come from the VM's own\n   123\t   `:module/require` park builder, the parking point every kernel supplies\n   124\t   for an effectful primitive.  The walker's response reader nests its\n   125\t   continuation in an `eval-call` frame (`ffi/call-response-wait-entry`);\n   126\t   the positional kernels wait on their register payload\n   127\t   (`ffi/response-wait-entry`).  The pair's call-in is a ring buffer, which\n   128\t   never answers `full`, so any outcome but `ok` fails the call.\"\n   129\t  [state effect {:keys [park-entry-fns]}]\n   130\t  (let [build (get park-entry-fns :module/require)\n   131\t        _ (when-not build\n   132\t            (throw (ex-info \"dao.space.query/q cannot park at this call site\"\n   133\t                            {:op op})))\n   134\t        {:keys [call-in]} (ffi/require-call-pair! (:resources state) op)\n   135\t        regs (build state effect nil)\n   136\t        call-id (ffi/call-id state (engine/park-id state))\n   137\t        walker? (= :ast-walker (:vm-model state))\n   138\t        parked (engine/park-continuation\n   139\t                 state\n   140\t                 (if walker?\n   141\t                   {:k {:type :dao.stream.apply/eval-call,\n   142\t                        :next (:k regs),\n   143\t                        :env (:env regs)},\n   144\t                    :env (:env regs)}\n   145\t                   regs)\n   146\t                 call-id)\n   147\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome\n   148\t                  (apply2/put-request! call-in\n   149\t                                       (apply2/request call-id op\n   150\t                                                       (vec (:args effect)))))]\n   151\t    (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n   152\t      (throw (ex-info \"dao.space.query/q request could not be appended\"\n   153\t                      {:op op, :outcome outcome})))\n   154\t    (blocked parked\n   155\t             (if walker?\n   156\t               (ffi/call-response-wait-entry call-id (:k regs) (:env regs))\n   157\t               (ffi/response-wait-entry regs call-id)))))\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\t(defn register\n   161\t  \"`registry` with the bridge's effect handlers: the session's\n   162\t   `:module/require`, and the `q` call.  The module itself is not\n   163\t   installed.\"\n   164\t  [registry]\n   165\t  (-> registry\n   166\t      (module/register-effect-handler :module/require require-handler)\n   167\t      (module/register-effect-handler ::call call-handler)))\n   168\t\n   169\t\n   170\t(defn waiting?\n   171\t  \"True while `vm` waits on a query call: a response reader or a retained\n   172\t   request writer of the call pair.\"\n   173\t  [vm]\n   174\t  (boolean (some #(or (some? (ffi/response-call-id %))\n   175\t                      (some? (ffi/request-call-id %)))\n   176\t                 (:wait-set vm))))\n   177\t\n   178\t\n   179\t;; =============================================================================\n   180\t;; The call pair\n   181\t;; =============================================================================\n   182\t\n   183\t(defn- medium\n   184\t  [capacity]\n   185\t  (:dao.stream/handle\n   186\t    (ring/create! {:dao.stream/type ring/transport-type\n   187\t                   ring/capacity-key capacity})))\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t(defn- newest\n   191\t  [handle]\n   192\t  (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor handle stream/anchor-newest)))\n   193\t\n   194\t\n   195\t(defn discard-answers\n   196\t  \"`vm`, rolled back to a base that has no call outstanding, reading its\n   197\t   call-out from the newest position of `pair`'s call-out.  The base's\n   198\t   own cursor stopped where its round began; every response since\n   199\t   answers a call of the abandoned run, which no call of the base can\n   200\t   claim, and a failed round that made more calls than the medium holds\n   201\t   would otherwise leave that cursor behind evicted responses, so the\n   202\t   next call would read a gap.  The call ids stay unique: the rollback\n   203\t   carries the abandoned run's id counter forward.  A VM without the\n   204\t   call-out cursor is returned unchanged.\"\n   205\t  [vm pair]\n   206\t  (if (contains? (:resources vm) vm/call-out-cursor-key)\n   207\t    (assoc-in vm\n   208\t              [:resources vm/call-out-cursor-key]\n   209\t              (vm/cursor-entry vm/call-out-stream-key\n   210\t                               (newest (:call-out pair))))\n   211\t    vm))\n   212\t\n   213\t\n   214\t(defn abandon-requests\n   215\t  \"`pair` with the interpreter's cursor past every request on its\n   216\t   call-in: the calls of a run the shell has abandoned are not answered.\"\n   217\t  [pair]\n   218\t  (assoc pair :cursor (newest (:call-in pair))))\n   219\t\n  